## Why This Is Asked
Design a scalable, auditable CDC ingestion that evolves schemas safely while preserving privacy and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Central schema registry with versioned contracts
- Idempotent upserts and MERGE semantics
- Dynamic schema evolution and tenant isolation
- Late data buffering and privacy-preserving BI views
- End-to-end testing and monitoring of contracts and lineage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: contract validation and upsert
function upsertEvent(event, registry) {
  const contract = registry.getContract(event.version, event.schemaKey);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  // perform idempotent merge into Iceberg
  mergeIntoIceberg(event, contract.primaryKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift in production and implement safe rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant data isolation and lineage end-to-end?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of batch ETL design with simple data contracts and observable bad data handling.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts and simple validation
- Canonical schema and Parquet lake storage
- Bad data routing with error codes
- Schema drift awareness and basic tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-batch ETL skeleton (Node.js) - not actual Spark
function transform(record) {
  const isValid = record.user_id && record.event_ts && ["click","view","purchase"].includes(record.action);
  if (!isValid) return { bad: true, error_code: "SCHEMA_VIOLATION" };
  return {
    user_id: String(record.user_id),
    event_ts: new Date(record.event_ts).toISOString(),
    action: record.action,
    context: record.context || {}
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the ETL with a tiny dataset and verify bad_data counts?
- How would you extend to handle additional fields while preserving the canonical schema?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, multi-tenant streaming graph pipelines with strict data contracts, lineage, and compliance controls in modern lakehouse setups.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming patterns: watermarking, lateness windows, fault tolerance
- Idempotent upserts in Iceberg with composite keys (tenant/device/neighbor)
- Schema evolution for nested attributes and backward compatibility
- Per-record lineage and auditability; GDPR delete handling via tombstones
- Multi-tenant isolation, access controls, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo upsert for edge state
function upsertEdge(existing, incoming) {
  const key = `${incoming.tenant_id}:${incoming.device_id}:${incoming.neighbor_id}`;
  if (!existing || existing.version < incoming.version) return incoming;
  return existing;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arrival handling across tenants?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect cross-tenant data leakage or drift?
